# Published image references for the eight services this repository builds.
#
# Why this file exists: the compose interpolations for these images used to
# carry a silent default (`${HILT_IMAGE:-ghcr.io/fil-forge/hilt:main}`), so a
# caller that forgot to pass an override booted a published image and never
# heard about it. They are now required (`${HILT_IMAGE:?...}`), which leaves
# `make up` — whose whole job is to run the published network locally — with
# nowhere to get a reference from. This file is that source: smelt/Makefile's
# COMPOSE passes `--env-file .env.published --env-file .env`, so these load
# first and a developer's own .env still wins.
#
# DO NOT add this file to smelt/embed.go. pkg/stack/extract.go walks all of
# EmbeddedFiles into the temp directory it then runs compose in, so an
# embedded copy would sit in the compose project directory during Go tests
# and silently reintroduce exactly the fallback this change removes — a test
# that forgot its image overrides would go green against :main again. Go
# tests that genuinely want the published network ask for it out loud, with
# stack.WithPublishedImages().
#
# The same list lives in pkg/stack/options.go (publishedImages). That
# duplication is deliberate: the Makefile shells out to `docker compose`
# directly, so a Go table cannot reach it.
#
# This file used to claim the two copies could not drift silently, because a
# missing entry fails at `compose up` naming the variable. They drifted
# anyway: indexing-service was added to the Go table and not here, so
# `make up` was broken for as long as the indexer had been in the stack.
# Nothing in CI runs `make up`, so "not silent" and "not noticed" were the
# same thing. `TestPublishedImageSetAgrees` in pkg/stack is what makes the
# claim true now. It is a Go test rather than a check-*.sh because the first
# attempt was a shell script that compared the lists as SETS, and so reported
# agreement on a tree where PIRI_IMAGE and HILT_IMAGE had been swapped here —
# each service booting the other's image. Pairing a variable to a reference
# means knowing which config field each reaches, which is Go semantics.

PIRI_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/piri:main
HILT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/hilt:main
INGOT_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/ingot:main
UPLOAD_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/sprue:main
DELEGATOR_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/delegator:main
SIGNER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/piri-signing-service:main
SWARF_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/swarf:main
INDEXER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/fil-forge/indexing-service:main
